Alloy: winemac.drv: count the cursor as pinned at the edge of the window it is confined to.

Looking around in a game could stop working left or right while up and down still worked. Games confine the cursor
with ClipCursor, often to their whole monitor or to a rect a little larger than their window. macOS confines it with
a window's mouse confinement rect, so Wine holds the cursor to the part of the game's rect inside the window. But
when deciding whether the cursor was pinned, which turns a move into a relative one carrying the mouse's own motion,
Wine tested the rect the game asked for. At the window's edge the next point was still inside that rect, so the moves
went out as absolute moves to the same place and the game saw no movement that way.

The confinement handler now reports the region the cursor is really held in, read back from the window so it follows
the window as it moves, and the pinned test and cursor clipping use it. A cursor resting within a point of the edge
counts as pinned too.

